Metrics:
How opentelemetry collect the metrics. Its one time activity, we will initilize the meter provider and all the codes will go through meter provider. Meter provider will have a meter which will have a metric instrumentation and capture the mesurement ie metrics. Then metric exporter will export to otel collector or backends.Metric instrumentation will have 4 fields name, kind, description, unit these unit and description are optional
Here Meter provider, meter, metric instrument all will come into picture only if we are using code based instrumentations.

Metric:
Numeric msururemets collected over time. Single mesurement collected at a moment eg cpu of LG at 5:34 PM or acumalated data ie number req/sec.
Kind:
It has below different kinds ie how it will collect the metrics
Counter - It will increase it ie no of errors over time it will increase
Async counter - Collect the aggregated metric on interval like 9 , 9:05 etc it will be in incremental
Updown counter - It will be in up and down eg how many no of tcp connection over time etc
Asyc updown counter - Same as updown by do it async like time and aggregated
Gauge - It wont aggregate or increase it only show one number ie %cpu at current time
Histogram - It will help for comparision ie on x axis like how many response count of 200 on y axis of count

Traces:
If we hit a api , if it travers to multiple layer for each layer it will create a span and it will show the duration and all span together is a trace (over all duration) ie what we see in Jaeger waterfall trace.
How opentelemetry collect the Traces. Its one time activity, we will initilize the Trace provider and all the codes will go through Trace provider. Trace provider will have a Tracer which will have a Spans instrumentation and capture the mesurement ie Spans. Then Span exporter will export to otel collector or backends.
Here Tracer provider, Tracer, Span instrument all will come into picture only if we are using code based instrumentations.

When we click on each span of a trace what details we see
Name: name of the span
Context: Trace id, span id etc
Parent span id - If the span is connected with other span that id
Start and end time - Overall duration of the span
Attribute: Key value pair , if we need to pass any custom info
Event: if we need to pass any additional details to span will passed in This
Status: unset and ok are succussful indication, error means the span is having error
Kind: help the trace to arrange the spans in order ie waterfall model.It has below state
Client - if call goes ie outbound call and its syncronous call
Server - If incoming call comes its also syncronous call
Internal : A span within the process
Producer: Async call which perform a job
Concumer: Async all perform the job of producer. 

Context Propagation:
When we run the api, if it traverse to multiple layer, the layer one context will be shared to layer 2 and so on which is called context propagation with this only we were able to see the waterfall model in traces.Here the context we refer is the each span conext details which read above.

Logs:
Opentelemetry will collect the application log but it will enhance it using log bridge api. All application logging service(log4j) will have log bridge api will use that and enhance the log to opentelemetry model log. What enhancement it will do is along with existing log it will append the trace id, span id ie tracing related details for more detailed analysis.

Baggage:
Its a key value pair, we can add the baggage data , so it will travers to all the downstream services. Eg if we add the user id in baggage, if user reported the they face slowness, we can filter based on user id and see why it was slow.Want to add multiple data in bagage it can be comma seperated. Baggage we usually add as header ie baggage header ie baggage: userid=123

OTLP and Collectors:
The generated and collected data are received , proceessed and exported to backend using the OTLP ie opentelemetry proctcol with collector ie proxy to backend services. OTLP is opentelemetry protocal to transmit the data to backend and we have other protacol to transmit the data to backend apart from OTLP which is developed by other source to export the instrume nted data.
OTLP:
Its a protacol to transmit the data from application to backend either direct or through proxy ie collectors. Its support gRPC and HTTP1.1 transport to send the data from app collected data to backend
gRPC:
Google remote procedure call. It used to communicate between service ie between micro services.Its internally uses http2 transport when communicating between 2 services.on grpc You define a service and method using a .proto file.
gRPC = Remote Procedure Call
"Remote" means another machine/service.
"Procedure call" means it feels conceptually like calling a function/method.

