SLI, SLO and SLA 
SLI - Service level indicator
SLO - Service Level objective
SLA - Service Level Agreement.

Eg : Our api if the user experiance RT of 200ms they will be happy and if they breach and until 300 ms they will still ok and use it and if it breached 300 ms they will be unhappy. So SLA is something that they are ok to have , if it breached then they need to pay penalty.So SLO is the happy threshold we need to try achieve internally.So SLI is measurement to define the reliability
SLI = ( Good events/Total events ) * 100
SLI=(900/1000)*100 = 90% and if we give that we will achive reliability of 99.99 then its not achieved.So SLI is to calculate the reliability. To calculate the reliability need to calculate for 4 weeks or 28 days. If 99% then downtime will be 7 hours, if 99.9 then 40 min, if 99.99 then 4 min, if 99.999 then 24 sec.
Error budget - 100% - Commited reliability target ie 100-99.9= 0.1% if its 0.1 then its acceptable limit.if 100-90% here 90 is achieved SLO then 10% is we achived which is not good.

Application availability SLO calc = good resp/valid resp *100
latency SLO calc = req under threshold ie <200 ms/total req * 100
error SLO calc = no of error code 4xx and 5xx/total all codes * 100

SLI - measurement
SLO - target
SLA - Commits
Error budget - Space between target and Perfect state

